  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Periods of rain today, perhaps a few \nthunderstorms in the south.\n\nArea of showers developing over far SWrn PA may slide just to\nour south as forcing dips S. However, earlier heavy rain and RAP\nprogs indicatee that at least areas S of the Turnpike, if not\nRte 22, could have some pockets of heavy rain. That is also the\narea that received the heaviest rain overnight. Uncertainty in \nplacement of heavy rain - if anywhere - in the CWA today will \nlean us away from a Flood Watch at this point in time. However, \nheavy rain is still possible.\n\nPrev...\n\nA weak area of sfc low pressure near the PA-OH border will push\neast through the day today, resulting in periods of rainfall. A\nthunderstorm or two can not be ruled out across the south. Rain\nwill be steadiest and heaviest in the morning, and trend \nlighter and more scattered through the day. Additional rainfall\namounts generally 0.10 - 0.50\" with localized higher amts\npossible in repeat heavier rainfall. \n\n-------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Rainy potential continues into much of this \nweek, although uncertainty remains in the details. On the cool \nside as well.\n\nA large high over Quebec will push southward on Monday, giving\nthe area a chance for dry conditions, especially across the \nnorthern tier. However, several CAMs are now indicating a batch \nof light rain will move into the Laurels from the west. Winds \nwill be from the E to NE on Monday, giving us our first taste of\ncold air damming with highs only in the 60s to near 70.\n\nHeading into Tuesday and Wednesday, the wedge of colder and \nmore stable air will deepen over the area thanks to building\nhigh pressure to the north. An upper trough approaching from the\nwest will push warmer air up and over this cold wedge, likely\nresulting in rain. In this cold air damming, high temps are \nexpected to be in the 50s to near 60. At this time, the highest \nPOPS are over the west and south, given an inverted trough. \n\nThursday and Friday may end up dry, as a coastal storm develops\nalong the Atlantic coast and wraps dry air around the western\nside. However, model guidance is still showing large spread in\nthe evolution of a coastal system regarding its forward speed\nand location. Slow moving rex block patterns can be particularly\ndifficult to forecast.",
